{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":975957,"num_turns":104,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"cba2fe0c-4b58-4813-90d6-98654b3df438","total_cost_usd":10.157417999999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":169,"cache_creation_input_tokens":166971,"cache_read_input_tokens":13196906,"output_tokens":75394,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":166971,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1375,"cache_read_input_tokens":206375,"cache_creation_input_tokens":529,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":529},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":3485,"outputTokens":15,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.00356,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":169,"outputTokens":75394,"cacheReadInputTokens":13196906,"cacheCreationInputTokens":166971,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":10.153857999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Rework cycle 5 handed off to review — board status `to-review`.\n\n## What the reviewer found, reproduced first\n\nBefore touching anything, I reproduced their case on both toolchains: a module with `go 1.99.0` gives `go mod edit -json` exit 0 and `go mod tidy` exit 1 with `go.mod requires go >= 1.99.0 (running go 1.25.x; GOTOOLCHAIN=local)`. The cycle-4 probe read step 2 as `tidy exit == 0`, so it scored a representable future release as ecosystem-invalid and failed P1 against its own class-2 classification. Their diagnosis was correct — the defect was in the probe, not the classifier.\n\n## The fix\n\nRead from this host's Go sources, not from memory: `cmd/go/internal/modload/modfile.go` raises `*gover.TooNewError` only *after* `modfile.Parse` returns and `gover.Compare(f.Go.Version, gover.Local()) > 0`, and `internal/gover` imports only `cmp`. Upstream keeps representability and the host gate apart and applies them in that order; an exit status is their conjunction and cannot be decomposed back.\n\nThe probe now lifts `internal/gover/gover.go` whole and `FromToolchain` verbatim out of the *probed* toolchain's own `GOROOT`, builds them with that toolchain, and measures representability directly — nothing transcribed by hand, SHA-256 of every lifted source printed, and the extractor refuses a `FromToolchain` that stops being self-contained. The real command is kept only as corroboration, with its outcome classified into accepted / too-new / rejected; too-new is recognised structurally (the echoed version must equal the value under test), not by a grep. The two must agree wherever the shape layer accepts the value.\n\nThe contract-level argument for the split: Curator already reaches the host relation at its own firing site — a future release is a class-2 comparison above the resolved triple, reported with a *derived canonical assertion*. Folding the host gate into the grammar would classify one fact twice under two payloads.\n\nTwo probe bugs fell out along the way: an inherited `GOROOT` was silently collapsing the two-toolchain run into one, and `toolchainVersion` didn't pin `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`.\n\n## Evidence\n\nGreen, standalone, real exit codes: validate 0 (42 schemas / 422 vector files), unittest 0 (29), go test 0, go vet 0, gofmt 0 no output, `git diff --check` 0, generate-vectors 0, probe `-semantic isolated` 0 (58 cases, zero divergences, zero crosscheck failures). Clean probe `9c752ea`: regenerate-check 0 twice, release-check rc.5 0. Regenerated `conformance/v1` and `release/1.0.0-rc.5.json` byte-identical to the accepted predecessor.\n\nReported as failing because it is: `make regenerate-check` **inside** the task worktree exits **1** — it diffs against `57c1f56` with the rc.5 candidate uncommitted. Determinism comes from the predecessor byte-comparison plus the clean probe, as in cycles 1–4.\n\nFive expected-red controls, all exit 1 for their named reason: `-semantic tidy-exit` (P1 violated by `1.26.0`, `1.99.0`, `1.99rc1` on both toolchains), `-red patch-prerelease-compared`, `-red c-equals-upstream`, injected broken link, injected retired stem. The last two were reverted; the file is byte-identical to its pre-probe copy and validate exits 0 after.\n\nThe three superseded classifications are now runnable controls inside the probe binary rather than hand-edited copies — a control that stops failing is the alarm.\n\nDelta is still exactly `CHANGELOG.md`, `decisions/0007`, `docs/compiled-build-toolchain-requirements.md`. Nothing staged, committed, published or pinned; no platform claim for rust/swift/kotlin/jdk; predecessor worktree untouched at `57c1f56`.","ttft_ms":1947,"ttft_stream_ms":1437,"time_to_request_ms":16,"type":"result","duration_ms":1129865,"uuid":"3e12e31d-799f-4be2-be45-8e2badaf6f6c"}
